1
00:04:56,842 --> 00:05:02,842
A star’s trek for life, liberty, and love.

2
00:09:53,684 --> 00:09:59,684
To Be Takei is a documentary film directed by Jennifer M. Kroot.

3
00:14:50,526 --> 00:14:56,526
Over seven decades, actor and activist George Takei journeyed from a World War II internment camp to the helm
of the Starship Enterprise, and then to the daily news feeds of five million Facebook fans.

4
00:19:47,368 --> 00:19:53,368
Join George and his husband, Brad, on a wacky and
profound trek for life, liberty, and love.

5
00:24:44,210 --> 00:24:50,210
Self, played by George Takei.

6
00:29:41,052 --> 00:29:47,052
Self, played by Brad Takei.

7
00:34:37,894 --> 00:34:43,894
Self, played by Walter Koenig.

8
00:39:34,736 --> 00:39:40,736
Self, played by William Shatner.

9
00:44:31,578 --> 00:44:37,578
Self, played by Leonard Nimoy.

10
00:49:28,421 --> 00:49:34,421
Self, played by Nichelle Nichols.

11
00:54:25,263 --> 00:54:31,263
George Takei ( tə-KAY; born Hosato Takei, April 20, 1937)
is an American actor, author and activist.

12
00:59:22,105 --> 00:59:28,105
He is known for his role as Hikaru Sulu, helmsman of the USS Enterprise in the
Star Trek franchise, and Galactus in The Super Hero Squad Show.

13
01:04:18,947 --> 01:04:24,947
Takei was born to Japanese-American parents, with whom he lived in
Tule Lake Segregation Center during World War II.

14
01:09:15,789 --> 01:09:21,789
He began pursuing acting in college, which led in 1965 to the role of
Sulu, to which he returned periodically into the 1990s.

15
01:14:12,631 --> 01:14:18,631
Upon coming out as gay in 2005, he became a prominent proponent of
LGBT rights and active in state and local politics.

16
01:19:09,473 --> 01:19:15,473
He has been a vocal advocate of the rights of immigrants, in part through his work on
the 2012 Broadway show Allegiance, about the internment experience.

17
01:24:06,315 --> 01:24:12,315
Takei spoke both English and Japanese growing up and
remains fluent in both languages.

18
01:29:03,157 --> 01:29:09,157
He has won several awards and accolades for his work on human rights and Japan–United States relations,
including his work with the Japanese American National Museum in Los Angeles, California.
